Upstream code
-------------

The overwhelming majority of this tree is NVIDIA's, unmodified, under its
original terms: dual MIT / GPL-2.0. See COPYING.

Where this fork modifies an upstream file, the file keeps its original
NVIDIA copyright header and licence. Those modifications are contributed
under the same terms.

To see exactly what was changed, diff against the release this was forked at
rather than against upstream's moving branch — `upstream/main` will have
advanced past 610.43.02 and the diff would then include NVIDIA's own later
changes as well as the fork's:

    git remote add upstream https://github.com/NVIDIA/open-gpu-kernel-modules.git
    git fetch upstream --tags
    git diff 610.43.02 --stat


Files added by this fork
------------------------

New files carry their own SPDX headers:

    reverse/**                                    MIT
    kernel-open/nvidia/nv-doorbell-watch.*        MIT
    kernel-open/nvidia-dbell/**                   GPL-2.0
    src/common/sdk/nvidia/inc/mc-trace.h          MIT
    docs/**                                       prose; see below

mc-trace.h sits inside NVIDIA's SDK header tree because that is where the
build expects it, but it is the fork's own file, not NVIDIA's.

kernel-open/nvidia-dbell/ is GPL-2.0 deliberately, not by preference. It
calls register_user_hw_breakpoint(), which the Linux kernel exports
EXPORT_SYMBOL_GPL, and a module must be GPL-compatible to link against
such a symbol.

Documentation under docs/ is prose about how the hardware and driver
behave. Where it quotes constants, structure layouts or class identifiers,
those come from the SDK headers in this tree and remain NVIDIA's.


Third-party work referenced
---------------------------

The kernel-side doorbell watchpoint in kernel-open/nvidia/nv-doorbell-watch.c
is a port of the technique described in:

    Yan et al. (2026), "Revealing NVIDIA Closed-Source Driver Command
    Streams for CPU-GPU Runtime Behavior Insight", sections 5.1-5.2.

The `mc` library and its Hopper QMD construction moved to the separate
https://github.com/maxime-peim/libmc repository. Its NOTICE records the
library's NVIDIA-source and Mesa NVK provenance.


Warranty
--------

None. This is research code that runs with root privileges and writes
directly to GPU hardware registers. It has been exercised on exactly one
configuration — H100 PCIe, Linux 6.8 — and nowhere else. Do not install it
on a machine whose uptime matters.
